Sval: an extension the user turned off on a site or host reaches nothing in its tabs

Each policy holds the sites and hosts the user turned the extension off on,
and the hosts they turned it back on at under them (blockedSites).
Content-script matching and ChannelWrapper refuse the extension in a
tab whose top-level host is off by the most specific of them: no content
script in any frame, no request the tab makes in webRequest or
filterResponseData. A frame's process need not hold the tab's top-level
document, so its host comes from the parent's window tree, or the frame
load's top-level principal, which the parent sets (SvalBlockedSites.cpp).

